From: pcm@world.std.com (Peter C. McCluskey)
Date: Sat, 3 Sep 1994 15:48:48 -0400
Subject: [#94-9-26] question for FAQ

 dkrieger@netcom.com  (Dave Krieger) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-9-3:
>I don't consider anyone "extropian" who isn't a (small-L) libertarian;

>  If you think it's OK for some criminal gang calling itself a
>government to push around others who are trying to mind their own business,
>then I think you're still The Enemy.

 I can't quite agree.
 While someone who is actively promoting Stalinism is definitely not an
Extropian, I am not willing to accept a litmus test which appears to
exclude the following people:
A) Someone who would like to abolish many of the existing functions of
government, but wants the government to use its power to accomplish
some egalitarian goal (such as the Reverse Polish Moon Treaty, or Hans
Moravec's support for an expanded social security system). (I'm not
defending those egalitarian follies, just pointing out that some otherwise
sane people whom I consider Extropians have advocated them).
B) Someone who thinks it would be nice to try creating a libertarian society,
but that it would be unwise to hold any beliefs about its desirability until
we have empirical data about how well libertarianism works in a large scale
industrial society. After all, anyone who understands the idea of spontaneous
order knows that predictions about how a hypothetical society will function
usually overlook something important.
C) Someone who believes that anarchocapitalism will be the best economic
system around the time of the singularity, but that it is better for societies
at different stages of technological development to have different economic
systems.

 I suggest 2 changes to the position you stated:
A) Replace the requirement that a person advocate a particular form of
government with a requirement that a person respect the importance of
spontaneous order in any functioning society.
B) Abandon the divisive boolean approach to classifying people's ideologies.
Use fuzzy logic instead. (E.g. "The First Extropian Squirrel holds only 60%
of the beliefs that I consider important to being an Extropian").
